<h2>Context</h2>

<p>A garment factory is a flow of material. Fabric and yarn arrive, move through inspection and external processing, enter cutting and stitching, and eventually become finished goods. When that flow is split across paper registers and spreadsheets, the central question, “where is this order?”, becomes slow and unreliable.</p>

<p>Reve Stitching uses ERPNext as the core platform. I did not build ERPNext itself. My work is implementation and customization: translating the factory’s real workflows into records, constraints, scripts, reports and screens that staff can use every day.</p>

<h2>Why ERPNext</h2>

<p>ERPNext provides a mature open-source base for stock and manufacturing, with the Frappe framework available for customization. That made it more practical than licensing a large enterprise suite or rebuilding common ERP foundations from scratch.</p>

<p>The standard system is deliberately general. The engineering work begins where a garment factory’s physical process stops matching the generic workflow.</p>

